---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { drafts, metrics } from '../data/samples';

const title = 'One essay, three times — clarity';
const description =
  'The same brief written three ways: an unedited agent draft, the same brief after a pass with the skill, and a version built from an interview with the author.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <header class="head">
    <div class="sheet head-in">
      <a class="brand" href="/">clarity</a>
      <span class="here" data-here aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <a class="repo" href="/">The rules</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section class="sheet masthead">
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col">
          <h1>One essay, three times.</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            The brief was the same each time: a short essay on what it means to be human. What
            changed was how much of the author was in the room.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            Every draft below is in the repository, under
            <a href="https://github.com/addyosmani/clarity/tree/main/samples">samples/</a>,
            along with the transcript the third one was built from.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sheet part" id="before" data-part="The draft">
      <div class="row"><div class="col"><h2>1. The unedited draft</h2></div></div>
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col prose">
          <p>
            Asked for a short essay with no other instruction, an agent produces this. It is
            fluent, well organised, and grammatical. It also contains nothing that anyone knew
            before reading it: no claim you could check, no detail only this writer had.
          </p>
          <p>
            The tells are the recognisable ones. It opens on <q>In today's rapidly evolving
            technological landscape</q>, attributes its central claim to <q>Experts argue</q>,
            supports its one empirical statement with <q>Studies show</q>, and closes on a
            sentence about the future belonging to those who can hold progress and human values
            in the same hand.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <p class="margin">Draft</p>
        <div class="col">
          <div class="specimen">
            {drafts.before.blocks.map((b) =>
              b.kind === 'p' ? (
                <p set:html={b.html} />
              ) : (
                <ul>{b.items.map((it) => <li set:html={it} />)}</ul>
              ),
            )}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sheet part" id="after" data-part="The rewrite">
      <div class="row"><div class="col"><h2>2. After a pass with the skill</h2></div></div>
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col prose">
          <p>
            The skill establishes the register first. This is an evocation, so it owes the
            reader images rather than a thesis. That step matters: an earlier attempt demanded
            a disputable claim from a piece about wonder and produced an argument against
            wonder, which was correct, cold, and worse than what it replaced.
          </p>
          <p>
            What the pass can do is trade unsourced gesture for things a reader can feel and
            check. The unnamed experts become Darwin on blushing in 1872. The vague appeal to
            consciousness becomes the arrector pili, the muscle that puffs a frightened cat and
            does nothing on us. The <q>Studies show</q> sentence was cut rather than sourced,
            which is what the skill asks for when a citation cannot be named.
          </p>
          <p>
            What it cannot do is supply an author. Every specific here is public. Anyone could
            look them up, which means anyone could have written this.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <p class="margin">Rewrite</p>
        <div class="col">
          <div class="specimen">
            {drafts.after.blocks.map((b) =>
              b.kind === 'p' ? (
                <p set:html={b.html} />
              ) : (
                <ul>{b.items.map((it) => <li set:html={it} />)}</ul>
              ),
            )}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sheet part" id="interview" data-part="The interview">
      <div class="row"><div class="col"><h2>3. After the interview</h2></div></div>
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col prose">
          <p>
            For the third version the skill refused to edit and ran its interview instead. Addy
            answered into a voice memo for about six minutes. The essay was then assembled from
            the transcript by cutting and reordering, never by rewording, because putting his
            material into the model's words is the operation that turns it back into anybody's
            writing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Roughly 87% of the three-word sequences in what follows appear verbatim in that
            recording. The linter flags three contrastive pivots in it. All three are his, and
            they are left alone.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <p class="margin">Interview</p>
        <div class="col">
          <div class="specimen">
            {drafts.interviewed.blocks.map((b) =>
              b.kind === 'p' ? (
                <p set:html={b.html} />
              ) : (
                <ul>{b.items.map((it) => <li set:html={it} />)}</ul>
              ),
            )}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sheet part" id="measured" data-part="Measured">
      <div class="row"><div class="col"><h2>What the linter says</h2></div></div>
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col">
          <div class="tablewrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Words</th><th>Sentence CV</th><th>Hedges /100</th><th>Anchors /100</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {metrics.map((m) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{m.stage}</th>
                    <td>{m.words}</td><td>{m.cv}</td><td>{m.hedge}</td><td>{m.anchors}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              The hedge column is the one worth looking at. Real prose hedges at roughly 3.5 to
              4.5 words per hundred. Neither model-written version gets near that, and the
              rewrite is the furthest away: stripped of hedges it reads cleaner than any person
              writes. The interviewed version lands at 4.45 with nobody aiming at it, because
              that is simply how someone talks. You cannot edit your way to that number.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sheet part" id="detector" data-part="A detector">
      <div class="row"><div class="col"><h2>One detector run</h2></div></div>
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col">
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              Out of curiosity the interviewed version went through Pangram, which scores text
              for whether a model wrote it. It came back as fully human.
            </p>
          </div>
          <figure class="shot">
            <a href="https://www.pangram.com/history/c14b5c31-6386-4e2e-b50f-52f1d6da4531?ucc=hhTApSdnbsM">
              <img
                src="/detector-result.png"
                alt="A detection tool reporting the interviewed essay as 100% human written."
                width="1600"
                height="842"
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
              />
            </a>
          </figure>
          <aside class="caveat">
            <p>
              <strong>This is illustrative and nothing more.</strong> It is a single run of one
              tool on one essay. Neither this skill nor the interview offers any guarantee about
              a detector score, and none of them is built to chase one. Detectors carry false
              positives and false negatives, they change without notice, and optimising prose
              against them makes the writing worse.
            </p>
            <p>
              The result is here for what it says about provenance rather than about scoring:
              the humanness came from the material being his, not from anything the edit pass
              did. The two model-written versions had the same rules applied.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sheet part" id="end">
      <div class="row">
        <div class="col prose">
          <h2 class="plain">What this is meant to show</h2>
          <p>
            An edit pass is worth having. It removed the stock openers, the unnamed experts, and
            the ending that would have fitted any essay in the category, and it put images where
            abstractions had been. None of that is nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            But the difference between the second and third versions is not craft. It is that
            somebody talked for six minutes about what they actually think, and the sentences
            they used survived into the piece. That is the part no rewrite can manufacture, and
            it is why the skill asks questions before it writes.
          </p>
          <p class="back"><a href="/">Read the eighteen rules</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 20; height: var(--head-h);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--bg) 82%, transparent);
    backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(150%);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .head-in {
    height: 100%; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }
  .brand { font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.01em; color: var(--fg-bright); text-decoration: none; }
  .here {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0; color: var(--fg-dim); font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase; white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis;
    opacity: 0; transform: translateY(0.3em);
    transition: opacity 0.32s var(--ease), transform 0.32s var(--ease);
  }
  .here[data-on] { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  .here[data-on]::before { content: '/\00a0\00a0'; color: var(--fg-faint); }
  .repo { color: var(--fg-muted); text-decoration: none; flex: none; }
  .repo:hover { color: var(--fg-bright); }

  .masthead { padding: clamp(3.5rem, 10vw, 6rem) 0 clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2.5rem); }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5.6vw, 3.1rem);
    font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.035em; color: var(--fg-bright);
  }
  .lede { margin: 1.4rem 0 0; font-size: 1.1875rem; color: var(--fg-muted); }
  .note { margin: 1rem 0 0; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--fg-dim); }
  .note :global(a), .back :global(a) { color: var(--fg-muted); }

  .part { padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4.5rem); }
  .part h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    padding-bottom: 0.9rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .part h2.plain {
    font-size: 1.3125rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; text-transform: none;
    color: var(--fg-bright); border: 0; padding: 0 0 0.6rem;
  }
  .row + .row { margin-top: 1.6rem; }

  .prose p { margin: 0 0 1.15em; }
  .prose p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .prose :global(q) { font-style: italic; color: var(--fg-muted); quotes: '“' '”'; }

  /* Specimens are exhibits, not the site's own voice. */
  .specimen {
    border-left: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    padding: 0.2rem 0 0.2rem 1.4rem;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.62;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }
  .specimen p { margin: 0 0 1em; }
  .specimen p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .specimen ul { margin: 0 0 1em; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .specimen li { margin-bottom: 0.4em; }
  .specimen li::marker { color: var(--fg-faint); }

  .tablewrap { overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 1.6rem; }
  table {
    width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse;
    font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
  }
  th, td { text-align: right; padding: 0.6rem 0.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  thead th { color: var(--fg-dim); font-weight: 500; font-size: 0.75rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em; }
  th:first-child, tbody th { text-align: left; color: var(--fg); font-weight: 500; white-space: nowrap; }
  tbody tr:last-child th, tbody tr:last-child td { color: var(--fg-bright); }

  .shot { margin: 1.6rem 0 0; }
  .shot img {
    width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong); border-radius: var(--r-md);
  }

  .caveat {
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    background: var(--bg-lift);
    padding: 1.15rem 1.25rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }
  .caveat p { margin: 0 0 0.8em; }
  .caveat p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .caveat strong { color: var(--fg-bright); font-weight: 600; }

  .back { margin-top: 2rem; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 0.9375rem; }
  main { padding-bottom: clamp(4rem, 10vw, 7rem); }

  @media (max-width: 46rem) {
    .part .margin { display: none; }
    .specimen { padding-left: 1rem; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  const label = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-here]');
  const parts = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-part]')];
  if (label && parts.length && 'IntersectionObserver' in window) {
    const seen = new Map<Element, boolean>();
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        for (const e of entries) seen.set(e.target, e.isIntersecting);
        const active = parts.filter((p) => seen.get(p));
        const current = active.length ? active[active.length - 1] : null;
        const name = current?.dataset.part ?? '';
        if (label.textContent !== name) label.textContent = name;
        label.toggleAttribute('data-on', Boolean(name));
      },
      { rootMargin: '-25% 0px -70% 0px' },
    );
    parts.forEach((p) => io.observe(p));
  }
</script>
